A number of people have said that the things I've suggested dropping
from Nethack are useful.  That's true.  I don't think there's
*anything* in Nethack that isn't useful to the careful adventurer.
But I think the things I've suggested for dropping are things of
marginal utility.  Treasure vaults, for instance, simply make
collecting gold easier, w/o adding anything new to the game.  Hawaian
shirts exist to let mega-gamers crank their AC to ridiculous levels (I
guess they don't realize that AC better than -10 is of little value).
And so on.  I think Nethack has gotten too cluttered with things that 
add little to the game, and I think some of these things can be dropped
without affecting the balance or the interest of the game.

Top Five New Artifacts to Add to Nethack

5.  Nets.  A weapon that can be thrown and will entangle if it hits
    for some length of time dependent on the creature's strength.  
    Breaking out of the net has a chance to destroy it.
4.  Rosaries (a type of amulet).  If you are wearing the correct type
    of rosary (i.e., lawful, chaotic, neutral), you'll be able to pray
    slightly more often, there's a chance that similarly-aligned
    monsters will become peaceful when they see the rosary, and you
    will have a chance to automatically turn undead.  Wearing an
    improper rosary would be very bad for your relationship with your
    god, but would not convert you or help you in any way.
3.  Wand of Wonder.  A wand that has random effects (except wishes).
    Wand of Draining.  A wand that drain half your hit points and
    does 2x that damage to the target.  A cursed wand of draining 
    drains half your hit points and does 1/4 to your target, blessed
    does 4x.
2.  Molotov Cocktail (a potion).  Indistinguishable from booze except
    with an identify, it explodes into flames and does fire damage 
    when thrown at a monster.  Cursed MCs explode in your hand when
    you try to throw them, doing damage to you.  Blessed MCs do damage
    to 8 squares ala scroll of fire.
1.  Faerie Dust.  The magic cure-all; a tool with limited charges.
    Faerie Dust sprinkled on an item removes a curse, quaffed it
    heals, thrown at monsters it blinds them with a sparkling spray,
    applied to a door or chest it unlocks, sprinkled into a fountain
    it turns the fountain into Galadriel's Mirror (i.e., the crystal
    ball effect), thrown on a shopkeeper it has a small chance to
    convince him that you've already paid your bill, sprinkled on an
    altar it changes the altar to a non-aligned glowing altar of the
    Faerie King, creates in the room a number of peaceful nymphs, and
    has a small chance to summon the Faerie King himself, who would
    grant the adventurer the Powers of Faerie (teleportation, speed,
    charming).  Faerie Dust can only be found on the Queen Nymph, who
    is in a special room (similar to a beehive) populated by centaurs
    and nymphs.  In addition to teleportation, speed and charming, the
    Queen Nymph can summon more nymphs/centaurs, make herself
    invisible, cast sleep, and is magic resistant.  If she summons,
    the Queen Nymph has a small chance of summoning the Faerie King
    himself, who will appear with a consort of guards and deal very
    harshly with the foolish mortal desecrating the Queen Nymph.  The
    Faerie King is roughly equivalent to a medium level demon.
    When other nymphs on the Queen Nymph level steal anything, it
    magically becomes the Queen Nymph's property.  The fifth time the
    Queen Nymph teleports she disappears forever, taking with her any
    property the nymphs have stolen.  There's a small chance based on
    charisma that when the Queen Nymph charms she'll be charmed
    herself and hand over her treasures (so to speak :-).  In addition
    to 3-8 charges of Faerie Dust, the Queen Nymph has a magic mirror
    and a cloak of magic resistance.

